### GreenSense — 02 · Fake IoT devices reporting in
#
# These are the HTTP-fallback equivalents of what an ESP32 publishes over MQTT. The
# handler is the same `TelemetryIngestControl` either way, so anything you can provoke
# here also happens on the broker path.
#
# For the MQTT versions of the same scenarios, see mqtt-scenarios.md in this folder, or
# run tools/device-simulator (which drives them continuously).
#
# Run 00-auth.http and 01-provisioning.http first.

# ── Cấu hình — sửa ở đây ────────────────────────────────────
# Giữ khớp với 00-auth.http. Biến khai ngay trong file, nên đổi là đổi tại chỗ — không
# phải đi tìm environment nào khác.
@baseUrl = https://green.visualedu.ai.vn
# @baseUrl = http://localhost:8080
# @baseUrl = http://159.65.4.47:8090

@email = green123@gmail.com
@password = Green@1508
@memberEmail = member@greensense.vn
@memberPassword = Green@123
@deviceCode = ESP32-A1

# {{accessToken}}, {{gardenId}}, {{pumpId}}, {{scheduleId}}, {{alertId}} KHÔNG khai ở đây:
# chúng được bắt từ response khi chạy 00-auth.http và 01-provisioning.http.
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# HAPPY PATH — everything inside its band
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

### A calm afternoon: nothing breaches, nothing fires
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/gardens/{{gardenId}}/readings/ingest
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "readings": [
    { "deviceCode": "{{deviceCode}}", "channel": "temp-1",  "value": 27.4 },
    { "deviceCode": "{{deviceCode}}", "channel": "hum-1",   "value": 65.0 },
    { "deviceCode": "{{deviceCode}}", "channel": "soil-1",  "value": 48.0 },
    { "deviceCode": "{{deviceCode}}", "channel": "lux-1",   "value": 850  },
    { "deviceCode": "{{deviceCode}}", "channel": "ph-1",    "value": 6.2  }
  ]
}

> {%
    client.test("all five readings accepted", function () {
        client.assert(response.body.data === 5, "expected 5 accepted, got " + response.body.data);
    });
%}

### A single reading with an explicit timestamp
# A node that buffered while offline replays with the ORIGINAL timestamp, not "now" —
# otherwise a two-hour outage collapses into one spike on the chart.
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/gardens/{{gardenId}}/readings/ingest
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "readings": [
    { "deviceCode": "{{deviceCode}}", "channel": "temp-1", "value": 26.8, "timestamp": "2026-08-12T03:00:00Z" }
  ]
}

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# THRESHOLD BREACH — raises an alert, drives the bell badge
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

### Too hot (31.5 °C, warnHigh is 30) → WARNING alert
# Run this twice inside 30 minutes and the second one raises NOTHING: identical alerts are
# de-duplicated per (code, sensor). That is why a probe reporting every 30 s while the plot
# is dry does not produce 120 notifications an hour.
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/gardens/{{gardenId}}/readings/ingest
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "readings": [
    { "deviceCode": "{{deviceCode}}", "channel": "temp-1", "value": 31.5 }
  ]
}

### Dangerously hot (39 °C, hard max is 38) → CRITICAL alert
# CRITICAL is also the severity that punches through quiet hours.
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/gardens/{{gardenId}}/readings/ingest
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "readings": [
    { "deviceCode": "{{deviceCode}}", "channel": "temp-1", "value": 39.0 }
  ]
}

### Back inside the band → the open alert is RESOLVED automatically
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/gardens/{{gardenId}}/readings/ingest
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "readings": [
    { "deviceCode": "{{deviceCode}}", "channel": "temp-1", "value": 27.0 }
  ]
}

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# AUTOMATION — a reading that starts hardware
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

### Dry soil (24 %, rule fires under 30) → pump starts, alert raised
# Check the result with 03-operations.http → "Actuator states": pump-1 should be ON with
# lastChangedBy=RULE, and the timeline should carry
# "Bơm nước luống 1-2 tự động bật / độ ẩm đất dưới 30%".
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/gardens/{{gardenId}}/readings/ingest
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "readings": [
    { "deviceCode": "{{deviceCode}}", "channel": "soil-1", "value": 24.0 }
  ]
}

### Harsh sun (25 000 lux) → curtain closes, but only between 09:00 and 16:00 local
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/gardens/{{gardenId}}/readings/ingest
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "readings": [
    { "deviceCode": "{{deviceCode}}", "channel": "lux-1", "value": 25000 }
  ]
}

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# FAULTY HARDWARE — the scenarios that must NOT drive automation
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

### Disconnected soil probe reads exactly 0 % → SUSPECT
# This is the important one. 0 % looks like "bone dry" and would start the pump; because
# the value sits exactly on the rail it is flagged SUSPECT instead, automation is skipped
# for that sensor, and a fault alert tells the operator to check the cable.
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/gardens/{{gardenId}}/readings/ingest
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "readings": [
    { "deviceCode": "{{deviceCode}}", "channel": "soil-1", "value": 0 }
  ]
}

### Probe pegged at full scale → also SUSPECT
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/gardens/{{gardenId}}/readings/ingest
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "readings": [
    { "deviceCode": "{{deviceCode}}", "channel": "hum-1", "value": 100 }
  ]
}

### Physically impossible temperature → BAD, stored for diagnosis only
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/gardens/{{gardenId}}/readings/ingest
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "readings": [
    { "deviceCode": "{{deviceCode}}", "channel": "temp-1", "value": -273.15 }
  ]
}

### pH out of the 0–14 range → BAD
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/gardens/{{gardenId}}/readings/ingest
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "readings": [
    { "deviceCode": "{{deviceCode}}", "channel": "ph-1", "value": 17.4 }
  ]
}

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# REJECTED INPUT
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

### Unregistered channel → accepted count is 0, nothing stored
# Hardware is never auto-provisioned from traffic: an unknown code is dropped and logged.
# Otherwise anyone who can reach the broker could invent sensors in your garden.
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/gardens/{{gardenId}}/readings/ingest
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "readings": [
    { "deviceCode": "ESP32-GHOST", "channel": "temp-99", "value": 25.0 }
  ]
}

> {%
    client.test("unknown hardware is dropped", function () {
        client.assert(response.body.data === 0, "expected 0 accepted, got " + response.body.data);
    });
%}

### Missing value → 400 with the offending field named
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/gardens/{{gardenId}}/readings/ingest
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "readings": [
    { "deviceCode": "{{deviceCode}}", "channel": "temp-1" }
  ]
}

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# A FULL DAY, COMPRESSED — paste-and-run to give the charts something to draw
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

### Overnight → morning → midday → evening, in one batch
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/gardens/{{gardenId}}/readings/ingest
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

